Now, induced emf, $e=-\frac{\Delta \phi}{\Delta t}=\frac{-\left(\phi_2-\phi_1\right)}{t}$
\begin{aligned}
e & =-\frac{\left(N B A \cos 0^{\circ}-N B A \cos 60^{\circ}\right)}{t}=\frac{-N B A\left(1-\frac{1}{2}\right)}{t} \\
& =\frac{-B A N}{2 t}=-\frac{2 \times 20 \times 100 \times 10^{-4}}{2 \times 0.2}=-1 \mathrm{~V}
\end{aligned}

$\therefore$ Magnitude of induced emf $=1 \mathrm{~V}$

A moving conductor is equivalent to a battery of emf $=\mathrm{v} \mathrm{B} \ell \quad$ (motion emf)
Equivalent circuit

\mathrm{I}=\mathrm{l}_1+\mathrm{l}_2

applying Kirchoff's law

\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{I}_1 R+I R-v B \ell=0 \\
& \mathrm{I}_2 R+I R-v B \ell=0
\end{aligned}

adding (1) \& (2)

\begin{aligned}
& 2 \mathrm{IR}+\mathrm{IR}=2 \mathrm{vB} \ell \\
& \mathrm{I}=\frac{2 \mathrm{vB} \ell}{3 \mathrm{R}} \\
& \mathrm{I}_1=\mathrm{I}_2=\frac{\mathrm{vB} \ell}{3 \mathrm{R}}
\end{aligned}

